Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State
Acute Urgent Care
Profound dehydration, Altered mental status, Hyperglycaemia >30 mmol/L, Hyperosmolality

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: AUC-035
Reason for Visit: Severe confusion, profound weakness, and extreme thirst developing over several days in an elderly diabetic patient.
Location: Nursing home
Present at Visit: Patient and facility nursing staff
Urgency Justification: The nursing facility reported an elderly patient with Type 2 Diabetes who has become progressively more confused and lethargic over the past week, with a capillary blood glucose reading above the meter's detectable limit ("HI"). Urgent clinical assessment was necessary to evaluate for severe dehydration and altered sensorium, as the insidious onset of Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State (HHS) carries a very high mortality rate if aggressive fluid resuscitation is delayed until routine daytime care.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: [Nurse reports] He has been incredibly thirsty for days, urinating constantly, and today he doesn't even know his own name and can barely open his eyes.
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a profound, insidious onset of dehydration and severe weakness that has been progressing over the last 5-7 days. The nursing staff report massive polyuria and profound polydipsia during the early part of the week, which has now transitioned to lethargy and a severe altered mental status. The patient is currently confused, disoriented to time and place, and extremely drowsy. There is no history of nausea, vomiting, or abdominal pain. A mild, untreated urinary tract infection is suspected as the precipitating event.
Functional Status: Bedbound, highly lethargic, unable to feed or hydrate independently.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of coma, acute renal failure, and vascular thrombosis due to extreme hyperviscosity.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 90/55 | HR: 112 | Temp: 37.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 95% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears profoundly dehydrated, gaunt, and lethargic, with deeply sunken eyes and dry, cracked lips.
Physical Exam: Dermatological exam reveals severely poor skin turgor (skin remains tented when pinched) and bone-dry mucous membranes. Cardiovascular exam demonstrates tachycardia and significant hypotension, confirming severe intravascular volume depletion. Neurological exam reveals confusion and sluggish responses, but no gross focal neurological signs (though focal signs or seizures can occur in HHS). Crucially, respiratory exam shows a normal respiratory rate without deep Kussmaul respirations, and there is an absolute absence of a fruity acetone breath odour. Expected investigations would show marked hyperglycaemia (>30 mmol/L), a serum osmolality >320 mOsm/kg, and an absence of significant ketonuria or acidosis.
Environmental Observations: Multiple empty water jugs; strong smell of concentrated urine in the room.
Medication Reconciliation: On Metformin and Gliclazide, but compliance has been poor due to underlying lethargy over the past week.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Diabetic Ketoacidosis (DKA) - Ruled out/unlikely due to the slow, insidious onset over days rather than hours, the absence of Kussmaul breathing, and the lack of a fruity acetone breath odor, indicating profound hyperglycemia without severe acidosis.

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out/unlikely due to the global nature of the altered mental status and confusion, combined with extreme hyperglycemia and dehydration, rather than a sudden onset of focal neurological deficits.

Severe Sepsis (e.g., Urosepsis) - Ruled out/unlikely as the sole cause, though a UTI is a likely trigger; the predominant features are the profound osmotic diuresis and "HI" glucose readings pointing directly to HHS.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient exhibits the classic clinical picture of a Hyperosmolar Hyperglycaemic State: profound dehydration, altered mental status, suspected extreme hyperglycemia, and normal respiratory mechanics without ketosis. The severe volume depletion, evidenced by hypotension and tachycardia, puts the patient at imminent risk of cardiovascular collapse, acute kidney injury, and fatal thromboembolic events. An urgent after-hours evaluation was absolutely necessary to identify the critical nature of this metabolic crisis. Immediate hospital transfer for massive, slow intravenous fluid resuscitation was mandated, preventing progression to irreversible coma.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Verified capillary blood glucose (Result: "HI"). Directed nursing staff to position the patient supine with legs elevated to support blood pressure. Immediately activated 000 for Priority 1 ambulance transport for intravenous fluid resuscitation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By ensuring strict monitoring of your blood sugar levels and increasing your water intake the moment you feel a minor infection starting, you prevent your blood from becoming dangerously thick and concentrated.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Ambulance transport to a major hospital.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Handover to paramedics; geriatric/endocrinology review required post-discharge.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. Nursing staff must monitor the patient's airway continuously. If the patient becomes completely comatose, roll into the lateral recovery position.

